{"flags": [
  {
    "id": "hejaz-railway-1908",
    "tags": ["region"],
    "problems": [
      "region: marked gulf, but culturalNotes say the line ran from Damascus through Syria and Jordan, so most of the route and its stations are Levant.",
      "The card mixes Ottoman Levant stations and Hejaz stations as if they were one place, and the imagery does not say which stations it means.",
      "culturalNotes: 'Credit the Hejaz Railway' credits the railway itself, not people."
    ],
    "fix": "Either mark the region mena and keep the whole Damascus to Medina line, or keep it gulf and limit imagery and artQueries to the Saudi section stations such as Hegra and Tabuk. Rewrite the Credit line to name the workers and the communities along the route."
  },
  {
    "id": "kuwaiti-chandal-ceiling",
    "tags": ["weak-copy", "credit"],
    "problems": [
      "This card nearly duplicates najdi-athl-wood-beams in the same chunk. Both build a header or list from a roof of parallel poles laid under a mat or rib layer, with the same 'lays' motion.",
      "culturalNotes: 'brought by sea trade' leaves out the documented source, the mangrove poles shipped from the East African coast by Gulf dhows.",
      "Credit: names only Gulf masons and carpenters, not the East African pole trade that supplied the chandal."
    ],
    "fix": "Set it apart from the Najdi card by building it on the dhow trade itself, for example the pole ends seen in the wall face and the bundled cargo poles. A tick or stack mechanic would fit this better than a ceiling band. Name the East African coast as the source and credit both the Kuwaiti builders and the sailors and pole cutters of that trade."
  },
  {
    "id": "kuwaiti-loomi",
    "tags": ["fact", "incoherent"],
    "problems": [
      "idea and palette: a black loomi splits to show a dark brown to black inside, not 'deep amber', so 'lime core amber' and the amber reveal are not true to the thing.",
      "name: 'Kuwaiti' makes it sound like a Kuwaiti thing, but dried lime is used across the whole Gulf and is most often linked to Oman. The card itself says 'Kuwaiti and Gulf kitchens'.",
      "palette: the 'fresh lime green' support color goes against the card's own avoid rule about losing the dried, dark character."
    ],
    "fix": "Rename it to 'Gulf Loomi' or 'Gulf Dried Lime', or keep Kuwait only for a narrower angle such as Kuwaiti loomi tea. Make the core reveal a dark brown center with a tan pith ring, and drop the fresh green for a dried tan or pith color."
  }
]}
